Local AWS sandbox
=================

This box has no route to AWS. Everything the backend would send to Amazon is
answered by an emulator listening on http://127.0.0.1:4566, which speaks the
same wire protocols the AWS SDK uses. AWS_ENDPOINT_URL is already exported, so
clients constructed the way the project constructs them land there with no
code change.

Credentials already in the environment belong to a bootstrap identity in the
metering account. The estate that carries the block storage is a separate
account, reached the way the gatherers reach it, by assuming a role:

    metering account   900000000001
    metered account    100000000077
    role               arn:aws:iam::100000000077:role/meteringco-metering
    external id        orchard-sbx-4f21
    business           biz-orchard
    default region     us-east-1

The metered dimension the sandbox is set up around is
dim-block-storage-sandbox, and dim-object-storage-sandbox also appears on some
volumes and snapshots.

The estate the emulator is currently serving is described by
/opt/metering-sandbox/public.json. It is a small, quiet estate: one metered
account and a couple of dozen volumes and snapshots spread over a handful of
regions. Real businesses hand us more accounts, more regions and messier
tagging, so treat what you see here as the easy case rather than the shape of
the world.

The emulator is region-aware. Every request is served against the region its
credentials were signed for, so the same call made with clients in two regions
returns two different answers. It will answer for any region name you send it,
including ones this account has nothing to do with; what an account is actually
switched on in is something only EC2 itself can tell you.

It also misbehaves the way AWS does. Inventory reads come back in short pages.
Some regions refuse a read outright with an authorization error, and which read
they refuse differs by region. At least one region rate limits the first few
calls of a read before letting it through, which is more attempts than the AWS
SDK gives a call by default.

To point the emulator at a different estate document, restart it yourself:

    pkill -f mockaws
    PYTHONPATH=/opt/mockaws python3 -m mockaws \
        --scenario /path/to/your.json --host 127.0.0.1 --port 4566 --seed 7 &

TypeScript in this project can be run directly with tsx, for example

    tsx -e "import('./src/utils/aws/awsEc2.js').then((m) => console.log(Object.keys(m)))"
